-- title:
--   Finitely generated equaliser ideal of two affine points
-- statement:
--   Let $Y$ and $S$ be schemes (in a fixed universe) and let $p : Y \to S$ be a morphism which is separated and locally of finite type. The assertion is the existence of a rule $I$ which, to every commutative ring $R$ (of the same universe) and every pair of morphisms $\sigma, \tau : \operatorname{Spec} R \to Y$ together with a proof $h$ that $\sigma$ followed by $p$ equals $\tau$ followed by $p$, assigns an ideal $I(R,\sigma,\tau,h) \subseteq R$, subject to three conditions. First, for all such $R, \sigma, \tau, h$ the ideal $I(R,\sigma,\tau,h)$ is the zero ideal if and only if $\sigma = \tau$. Second, $I$ is compatible with base change along rings: for all commutative rings $R, R'$, every ring homomorphism $\psi : R \to R'$ and every $\sigma, \tau, h$ as above, the ideal attached to the two composites $\operatorname{Spec}\psi$ followed by $\sigma$ and $\operatorname{Spec}\psi$ followed by $\tau$ (which agree after composing with $p$, by associativity and $h$) equals the ideal of $R'$ generated by the image of $I(R,\sigma,\tau,h)$ under $\psi$. Third, every ideal $I(R,\sigma,\tau,h)$ is finitely generated.
--
--   This is the equaliser-ideal construction for a pair of $S$-valued affine points of $Y$: $I(\sigma,\tau)$ cuts out the pullback along $(\sigma,\tau)$ of the diagonal of $p$, which is a closed immersion because $p$ is separated and is moreover of finite presentation because the diagonal of a morphism locally of finite type is locally of finite presentation, whence finite generation. It is used in the study of polarised abelian schemes, where the locus on which two points agree must be represented by a finitely generated ideal, in [`AlgebraicGeometry.PolarisedAbelianScheme.exists_isClosedImmersion_iff_exists_level_generator_lfp`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.PolarisedAbelianScheme.exists_isClosedImmersion_iff_exists_level_generator_lfp).
